"""Model answers for the 9-mark levels-of-response questions (and two essays).

For each question: a Level 3 answer, a weaker answer, and notes on what separates them.
Written from the same textbook pages as the question (see the question's page citation);
every point in the model answers appears in the mark scheme's indicative content.

top / low are HTML fragments (<p> paragraphs)."""

EXEMPLARS = {
    "PCON-08": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Deciding how much to control pollution starts with predicting where a pollutant will go. Critical Pathway Analysis uses the state of the effluent, wind and currents, geology, the effect of pH and oxygen on solubility, bioaccumulation and local food sources to predict whether a discharge will disperse harmlessly or concentrate somewhere sensitive; it is mainly used for radioactive discharges and means monitoring can focus on the sites at risk rather than sampling everywhere. Critical Group Monitoring then checks the exposure of the people most at risk from their lifestyle before any health effects occur: if their risk is acceptable everyone else's is lower, and if not, emissions are reduced.</p><p>The choice of control is guided by principles. The polluter pays principle gives a financial incentive to prevent damage, but only if penalties are high and polluters cannot escape responsibility. The precautionary principle assumes a waste is harmful until research shows otherwise, so ignorance is no defence. Because the cost of control rises steeply as emissions approach zero, emissions are set As Low As Reasonably Achievable using the Best Available Technology Not Entailing Excessive Cost. Where emission location and timing can be chosen (marine outfalls, downwind of towns, not over aquifers; avoiding temperature inversions) severity is reduced cheaply.</p><p>Overall, prevention of production (fuel desulfurisation) or of release (electrostatic precipitators, catalytic converters) is more effective than post-release remediation such as oil-spill clean-up, which never recovers all the pollutant. CPA depends on understanding pathways, and transboundary pollutants need international enforcement, but together these principles give a rational basis for deciding acceptable risk rather than aiming for an unaffordable zero.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Pollution can be controlled in lots of ways. Critical Pathway Analysis looks at where pollution goes and Critical Group Monitoring looks at people. The polluter pays principle means the person who pollutes has to pay. The precautionary principle means being careful. Pollution can be prevented, or cleaned up afterwards with things like booms for oil spills. Electric cars are an alternative process. Pollution control can be expensive so ALARA is used. All of these are good ways of controlling pollution.</p>",
        "notes": ["The Level 3 answer explains <i>how</i> each principle works (why penalties matter, why costs rise near zero emissions); the weak answer only names them.",
                  "The Level 3 answer groups the material logically (predict, decide, select) rather than listing everything at the same weight.",
                  "'Evaluate' needs comparison and a judgement: prevention vs remediation, CPA's dependence on understanding pathways, and a conclusion about acceptable risk. The weak answer ends with 'all of these are good'.",
                  "Precise examples (desulfurisation, precipitators, catalytic converters, temperature inversions) show applied knowledge; 'things like booms' does not."]},
    "RM-21": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>A valid investigation begins with a clear, testable hypothesis and a null hypothesis, informed by existing knowledge, so that the data collected can actually accept or reject it. The first design decision is sample location: random sampling (grid coordinates or numbered sites) or systematic sampling along transects avoids the bias that would come from choosing convenient sites, and a preliminary study shows the interval needed to detect variation without collecting unnecessary data.</p><p>Timing matters because many variables change diurnally, seasonally or with weather, so samples on different occasions are needed and the interval must match the rate of change. Sample size and number depend on how variable the population is: a running mean shows when enough samples have been taken and the standard deviation shows the scatter around the mean. Techniques must be standardised so results from different sites, times or researchers are comparable, other variables controlled or monitored, and equipment calibrated so that precise readings are also accurate; anomalies should be repeated rather than ignored.</p><p>Finally the analysis must be chosen to suit the data - Spearman's rank for correlation, chi-squared for frequencies, a t-test for two means or Mann-Whitney U for medians - so that significance (p &lt; 0.05) can be assessed, remembering that a significant correlation does not prove causation. Every decision trades reliability against cost, time and disturbance; sub-samples can only estimate the whole, so the most valid study is the one whose sampling design makes its limitations explicit.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>When planning fieldwork you need a hypothesis. You should choose sites randomly so it is not biased, for example using random numbers. You need to take enough samples and repeat them so the results are reliable. The method should be the same each time. You should measure accurately with calibrated equipment. Then you do a statistical test such as a t-test to see if the results are significant. If p is less than 0.05 the result is significant. This makes the conclusions valid.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 is sound but generic: it says 'enough samples' where Level 3 explains how a running mean and standard deviation tell you how many.",
                  "Level 3 covers timing and the choice between statistical tests; Level 2 mentions one test only.",
                  "The evaluation is missing from Level 2 - there is no discussion of trade-offs or of correlation vs causation, so it cannot reach the top level however accurate it is."]},
    "BIOV-08": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Other species supply resources we use directly: wood, fibres such as cotton and wool, oils, fuels and food. Because most farmed species were domesticated long ago and are often kept where they are poorly adapted, wild species such as the drought-resistant morama bean, the yeheb tree that grows on poor soils, perennial maize that needs no ploughing, and bison that graze grassland too poor for cattle offer new, better-adapted food sources. Wild species also carry genetic resources: crop wild relatives in Vavilov centres hold genes for disease resistance and salt tolerance that inbred commercial varieties lack.</p><p>Biomimetics copies adaptations into technology - humpback flipper tubercles in turbine blades, shark skin in friction-reducing coatings, termite-mound convection in naturally ventilated buildings, hollow bird bones in lightweight roofs. Medicines such as taxol, quinine, AZT and diosgenin came from wild species, and physiological research on marsupials, hippo skin and sponge proteins is helping with foetal development, burns and transplant rejection. Since only a tiny fraction of species has been studied, the unknown future value argues for conserving whole habitats.</p><p>Beyond resources, other species provide services we could not replace: regulation of atmospheric gases, evapotranspiration driving rainfall, the carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus cycles run by microbes, soil formation by decomposers, and pollination, seed dispersal and habitat provision. These are unnoticed precisely because they are in dynamic equilibrium, so their economic value is underestimated. On balance the resource and service arguments alone justify conservation, but they are strongest when combined with the intrinsic and aesthetic case, because many species with no known use still sustain the systems we depend on.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Biodiversity is important because we get lots of things from other species. We get wood, cotton and food from plants and animals. Some medicines come from plants, like aspirin from willow trees. Animals can help design things, like Velcro was copied from seeds. Other species also do things for us like bees pollinating crops. If we lose species we might lose things that could be useful in the future. So we should conserve biodiversity.</p>",
        "notes": ["Both answers cover resources, medicines, biomimetics and services, but Level 3 develops each with specific, accurate detail (which species, what property, why it matters).",
                  "The question asks 'to what extent'. Level 3 weighs resource arguments against intrinsic value and reaches a reasoned conclusion; Level 1 asserts 'so we should conserve'.",
                  "Level 3 explains <i>why</i> services are undervalued (dynamic equilibrium) - an explanation, not just a list."]},
    "SUS-16": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Natural systems have remained sustainable because they run on low energy-density renewable energy, mainly sunlight driving the hydrological, carbon and nitrogen cycles; their reactions occur at low temperatures because enzymes lower activation energies; materials cycle so the waste of one process is the raw material of the next; only a few abundant elements are used; and wastes are biodegradable and non-toxic. Negative feedback keeps them in dynamic equilibrium and high diversity makes them resistant to change.</p><p>Human systems contrast on every point: non-renewable fossil fuels, high-temperature processes such as the Haber process and incineration, linear use that disperses minerals and mixes materials that cannot be separated, and persistent toxins like PCBs and the metals in electronics. We also override negative feedback - when degraded soil reduces yields we add fertiliser, irrigation and pesticides - and trigger positive feedback such as permafrost melting.</p><p>Copying natural principles is already possible in many areas: renewable energy and heat pumps, bioleaching at low temperature, recycling and design for disassembly, industrial symbiosis, legumes and organic matter instead of manufactured nitrate, and retaining hedgerows for pest predators - the basis of the circular economy. The limits are population size and affluence, the attitude of urban consumers who never see depleted resources, cost, and the need for international cooperation. So the principles can make individual activities far more sustainable, but only a large-scale, cooperative shift in lifestyle would make the whole human system sustainable.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Natural systems use renewable energy from the Sun and recycle materials, so nothing runs out and there is no waste. Humans use fossil fuels which will run out and produce waste that is toxic. Natural processes happen at low temperatures but the Haber process needs high temperatures. Humans could copy natural systems by using renewable energy like solar and wind, recycling more and using less fertiliser. This would make human activities more sustainable. However it would be expensive and people would have to change their lifestyle.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 states the contrast correctly but does not explain the mechanisms (enzymes, negative feedback, diversity, dispersal of minerals) that Level 3 uses.",
                  "Level 3 applies the principles to specific technologies (bioleaching, heat pumps, industrial symbiosis, legumes); Level 2 stays at 'recycle more'.",
                  "'Evaluate the extent' - Level 3 distinguishes individual activities from the whole system in its conclusion; Level 2's evaluation is a single sentence about cost."]},
    "MON-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>New technologies have transformed what can be monitored and how often. Image databases of unique markings - tiger stripes, whale-shark spots, dolphin fin scars - let individuals be recognised, giving territory size, movements, lifespan and social structure without repeated capture, though only for species with unique features. Motion-sensitive and infrared cameras and CCTV record activity day and night with far less disturbance than observers, especially at nests.</p><p>Genetics adds detection where nothing can be seen: eDNA in water reveals great crested newts or invasive carp from shed cells, and DNA databases identify which regional population timber, ivory or fish came from, supporting enforcement. Bat detectors and sonograms give presence, abundance and activity of species heard but not seen. Position monitoring uses radio, GPS and acoustic transmitters, or tiny geolocator tags for birds too small to carry a transmitter, while data recorders on drones, AUVs, balloons, aircraft, satellites or the animals themselves collect abiotic data. Satellites map land use and deforestation in visible light, vegetation density and temperature in infrared, and wind, waves, sea level and oil slicks with radar that works through cloud.</p><p>The gains are coverage, continuity and reduced disturbance. The limits are cost, battery life and weight, the need to calibrate sensors and ground-truth remote data, ethical questions about tagging, and the fact that many species still require traditional methods and indirect evidence. New technology therefore complements rather than replaces fieldwork, but for rare, remote or nocturnal species it has made effective conservation possible where it was not before.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>New technologies help monitor wildlife. Cameras can take pictures of animals at night. GPS collars can track where animals go. Satellites can see deforestation from space. DNA can tell which animals are present. Bat detectors can hear bats. These help conservation because we know where animals are and how many there are. They can be expensive though.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 lists technologies with no detail of how they work or what data they give; Level 3 links each to the information it provides (territory size, presence, activity).",
                  "Level 3's evaluation considers several limitations (calibration, ground-truthing, ethics, species coverage) and reaches a balanced judgement; Level 1 has one word - 'expensive'.",
                  "Structure: Level 3 groups by imagery, genetics, sound, position and remote sensing; the list in Level 1 has no organisation."]},
    "AIR-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Smoke is particulate matter from incomplete combustion of coal, diesel, wood and wastes. Its effects follow from particle size: PM10 and smaller particles stay suspended, travel far and penetrate the lungs, carrying acids, phenols and metals; smoke also reflects sunlight (cooling), blocks light for photosynthesis and blackens buildings. Under a temperature inversion in a valley it accumulates as smog, and it acts synergistically with sulfur dioxide - the London smog of 1952 killed about 12 000 people. Acid rain gases are different: sulfur dioxide from coal combustion and sulfide smelting, and nitrogen oxides from high-temperature combustion, are reactive and soluble, forming sulfuric and nitric acids that travel long distances across borders. Low pH denatures proteins in exposed cells such as gills, eggs and root hairs, dissolves exoskeletons and limestone, corrodes metals and leaches calcium and magnesium from soils while mobilising toxic aluminium, with severity depending on bedrock.</p><p>Smoke control has been the clearest success: the Clean Air Act's smokeless zones, the switch to gas and electricity, electrostatic precipitators, cyclones, bag filters and diesel particulate filters have virtually eliminated smoke smogs in developed countries. Sulfur dioxide has also fallen dramatically through fuel desulfurisation, flue-gas desulfurisation and the move from coal to gas and renewables, and lake pH is recovering, although FGD is costly and produces waste. Nitrogen oxides remain the hardest: catalytic converters and fluidised-bed combustion work, but converters need unleaded fuel and warm engines and vehicle numbers keep rising. Controls have therefore succeeded for particulates and sulfur, only partly for nitrogen.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Smoke is made of small particles from burning coal and diesel. It causes breathing problems and smogs like in London in 1952. Acid rain is caused by sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides which dissolve in rain and make it acidic. Acid rain kills fish and trees and damages buildings. Smoke is controlled by smokeless zones and filters. Acid rain is controlled by taking the sulfur out of fuel and catalytic converters in cars. These methods have reduced pollution a lot, especially smoke.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 3 links each effect to a property (particle size, solubility, reactivity, pH denaturing proteins); Level 2 describes effects without the mechanism.",
                  "Level 3 evaluates each control separately with evidence (recovering lake pH, cost of FGD, why NOx is harder); Level 2 gives one general sentence.",
                  "Both answers use the 1952 smog, but Level 3 explains the conditions (inversion, synergism with SO<sub>2</sub>) that caused it."]},
    "OZ-04": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Ozone depletion followed the scientific method almost textbook-fashion: Antarctic UV and ozone measurements identified a problem, Rowland and Molina's 1974 hypothesis explained it, Dobson-unit, satellite and balloon data confirmed chlorine monoxide in the stratosphere, and the Montreal Protocol enacted a solution. It has largely worked: CFC concentrations are falling, the ozone hole has stabilised and is starting to shrink, and global ozone loss was limited to about 4%.</p><p>Success came from particular conditions. The consequence - skin cancer - was clear and frightening, nearly every country agreed, targets were legally binding with a fund to help developing countries, there were relatively few producers and products, and alternatives (HCFCs, HFCs, hydrocarbons, pump sprays) already existed, so substitution cost little. Even so recovery will take decades because CFCs are so persistent, some ozone-depleting substances are still in use, and the HFC replacements are themselves greenhouse gases.</p><p>Climate change has none of these advantages. Greenhouse gases come from almost every activity - energy, transport, agriculture, industry - in every country, and fossil fuels underpin entire economies, so control means changing lifestyles and economies rather than swapping one chemical for another. Kyoto and Paris set weaker, largely non-binding targets, developed and developing nations dispute responsibility, and natural fluctuations, time lags and tipping points make cause and effect less obvious to the public. Emissions are still rising. The ozone story shows international agreement can work, but it was a technical substitution problem; climate change is systemic, so its lessons transfer only partly.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (4 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Ozone depletion was caused by CFCs. Scientists found the hole over Antarctica and the Montreal Protocol banned CFCs, so the ozone layer is now recovering. This was successful because everyone agreed and there were alternatives to CFCs. Climate change has not been tackled as well. There have been agreements like Kyoto and Paris but carbon dioxide is still going up. This is because fossil fuels are used everywhere and countries do not agree. So ozone depletion was solved better than climate change.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 gives the right outline but Level 3 supports it with evidence (falling CFC levels, 4% loss, Dobson units, ClO detection) and explains each reason for the difference.",
                  "Level 3 acknowledges the limits of the ozone success (persistence, HFCs) - balance is expected at the top level.",
                  "The final judgement in Level 3 characterises the difference (substitution vs systemic problem); Level 2 just restates the question."]},
    "BGC-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Humans have shifted the carbon cycle by burning fossil fuels and biomass, moving carbon from long-term stores into the atmosphere faster than photosynthesis and ocean uptake remove it, while deforestation reduces photosynthesis, ploughing and drainage speed aerobic decomposition, and paddies, landfills and livestock create new anaerobic sources of methane. The nitrogen cycle has changed as much: the Haber process has roughly doubled fixation, legume crops add more, drainage increases nitrification and reduces denitrification, combustion produces NOx, and leached fertiliser and sewage nitrate cause eutrophication, while nitrous oxide adds to warming.</p><p>Carbon can be counteracted by replacing fossil fuels with renewables and nuclear power, energy conservation, afforestation matched to deforestation and timber use, conserving peat bogs and forests, and increasing soil organic matter through low tillage and mulching, with carbon capture and storage as an unproven option. For nitrogen, catalytic converters and urea sprays reduce combustion NOx, biological fixation by legumes and rotation can replace manufactured nitrate, biological wastes can be managed as fertiliser, and low-solubility fertilisers, careful timing and buffer strips reduce leaching.</p><p>Some strategies are cheap and established - rotation, buffer strips, fertiliser timing - but others are expensive or untested, and the scale of fossil-fuel dependence, together with feedback and time lags, means the atmosphere will respond slowly. The strategies that mimic natural low-temperature cycling, as in the circular economy, are the most sustainable; the most effective is simply to leave carbon in the ground.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Humans have changed the carbon cycle by burning fossil fuels which releases carbon dioxide, and by cutting down trees. They have changed the nitrogen cycle by using fertilisers which run off into rivers and cause eutrophication. To counteract this we could use renewable energy instead of fossil fuels and plant more trees. We could use less fertiliser. These would help the cycles go back to normal.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 names one or two changes per cycle; Level 3 covers several processes for each (decomposition, methane sources, Haber process, nitrification, NOx).",
                  "Level 3 connects each counter-strategy to the process it reverses; Level 1's strategies are general.",
                  "The evaluation in Level 3 distinguishes established from unproven strategies and comments on scale and time lags - Level 1 has no evaluation."]},
    "CC-10": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Strategies fall into control of greenhouse gases, geoengineering, international agreements and adaptation. Emissions are cut by energy conservation in transport, buildings and industry, by replacing fossil fuels with renewables and nuclear, by carbon capture and storage, by reducing deforestation and planting forests, and by capturing methane from landfill. Geoengineering ideas - white roofs, ocean fertilisation, solar shades - remain largely untried with unpredictable side effects. Adaptation, such as flood defences and managed retreat, reduces impacts but not the cause.</p><p>The Kyoto Protocol and Paris Agreement set targets, but they are weak or non-binding and participation is uneven; carbon trading and taxes exist but per capita footprints still differ enormously between countries. The evidence of success is limited: atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> has risen from about 280 ppm before industrialisation to over 410 ppm and temperature by about 1 &deg;C, and although emissions are falling in some developed countries they are rising in developing economies.</p><p>The difficulty is that fossil fuels underpin economies, so control demands lifestyle change, cost, and political agreement between nations with different priorities, while time delays and natural fluctuations mask the effect of any action. By contrast the Montreal Protocol succeeded because ozone was a substitution problem. The judgement must be partial success: the technology exists and regional reductions prove it works, but global strategies have not yet stopped the rise, and tipping points make the delay dangerous.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Methods to reduce climate change include using renewable energy such as wind and solar instead of fossil fuels, saving energy, and planting trees to absorb carbon dioxide. International agreements like the Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement have set targets for countries to reduce emissions. However carbon dioxide levels are still rising, so the methods have not been very successful so far. This is because countries rely on fossil fuels and do not always keep to the agreements. More needs to be done.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 covers the main strategies and reaches a conclusion, but with no quantitative evidence (280 to 410 ppm, 1 &deg;C) and no mention of geoengineering or adaptation.",
                  "Level 3 explains <i>why</i> success is limited (economic dependence, time lags, natural variability, political disagreement) rather than just stating that it is.",
                  "The Montreal comparison and the tipping-point point give Level 3 the breadth and judgement the top band requires."]},
    "ENF-06": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Whether a resource suits a use depends on its features: renewable or not, abundance, energy density, locational constraints, intermittency and predictability, the conversions needed, ease of storage and transport, how developed the technology is and its impacts. Fossil fuels dominate because they combine high energy density, easy storage and transport, high temperatures for industry and mature infrastructure - which is why petrol and diesel still monopolise transport. But they are finite, cause acid rain, smoke and CO<sub>2</sub>, and are politically concentrated. Nuclear fuel has the highest energy density and gives reliable base load with few deliveries, yet the technology is complex and expensive and produces long-lived waste against public opposition.</p><p>Renewables have the opposite profile: low energy density needing large collectors, intermittency (wind and solar unpredictable, tidal predictable), strong locational constraints and no direct storage, so they need conversion, storage and grid links and cannot yet reach fossil-fuel temperatures or replace liquid fuels. Sustainability must count impacts before use - extraction, processing, embodied energy of equipment - as well as pollution, habitat damage and depletion during use, and economics and politics (subsidies, planning, external costs left out of prices, energy security) shape what is actually chosen.</p><p>No single resource is suitable for everything. Electricity, heat and transport have different requirements, so the most sustainable system is a diverse mix matched to each use with storage, and the transition must begin before depletion forces it.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (2 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Energy resources have different features. Fossil fuels are non-renewable and will run out but they have a high energy density. Renewable energy like wind and solar will not run out but they are intermittent because the wind does not always blow. Nuclear power produces radioactive waste. Renewable energy is more sustainable because it does not produce carbon dioxide. So we should use more renewable energy.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 mentions two or three features; Level 3 works through the full set and links each to a specific use (transport, base load, industry).",
                  "Level 3 includes sustainability concepts (impacts before and during use, external costs) and political factors that Level 1 omits entirely.",
                  "The conclusion in Level 3 is a reasoned position (a diverse mix matched to uses); Level 1's 'use more renewables' does not follow from a discussion."]},
    "EST-06": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Renewable electricity is intermittent - solar stops at night, wind on calm days, tidal power at slack water - and some of it is unpredictable, while demand fluctuates by the minute, day, week and season, so supply and demand rarely coincide. Without storage, surplus renewable output is wasted and shortfalls must be met by fossil-fuel peaking plants, which defeats the purpose. Electricity itself cannot be stored at scale, so the energy must be converted into another form and back.</p><p>Pumped-storage HEP is proven and responds within seconds for peak shaving, but suitable sites are few. Lithium-ion batteries now work at grid scale and in vehicles, with vehicle-to-grid schemes turning car batteries into distributed storage, though lithium supplies and lifetime limit them. Hydrogen from electrolysis can be stored, used in fuel cells or injected into the gas grid (power-to-gas), and its high energy density suits vehicles, although each conversion loses energy. Compressed air with heat recovery, molten-salt thermal stores for concentrating solar plants, thermal mass in buildings, flywheels and supercapacitors each fit particular timescales.</p><p>Storage is not the only answer: a diverse mix of wind, solar, tidal and biofuel smooths supply, smart-grid management shifts demand, and conservation reduces peaks. But these reduce rather than remove the mismatch. Short-term storage can be batteries and pumped hydro; the harder problem is seasonal storage, where only hydrogen or fuels made from surplus electricity look plausible. Without both, renewables can supplement but not dominate a country's supply.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Renewable energy is intermittent. Solar panels do not work at night and wind turbines do not work when it is not windy, but people still need electricity then. Storage means the energy can be saved for when it is needed. Pumped storage pumps water uphill when there is spare electricity and lets it flow back through turbines at peak times. Batteries can also store electricity. Hydrogen can be made from electricity and used in fuel cells. So storage is needed for renewables to provide most electricity.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 explains the mismatch and describes three storage methods correctly, but does not evaluate them (sites, lithium, conversion losses, timescales).",
                  "Level 3 distinguishes short-term from seasonal storage - the key insight for 'most of a country's electricity'.",
                  "Level 3 also considers the alternatives to storage (diversity, grid management, conservation) and explains why they are insufficient alone."]},
    "ATM-06": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>In the troposphere human activity enhances the greenhouse effect: fossil fuels and deforestation add CO<sub>2</sub>, rice, livestock and landfill add methane, fertilisers add nitrous oxide, and CFCs and tropospheric ozone are new absorbers. More outgoing infrared is absorbed and re-emitted, warming the surface, while smoke and aerosols scatter sunlight and land-use change alters albedo and evapotranspiration. In the stratosphere, CFCs, halons and HCFCs release chlorine and bromine that catalyse ozone destruction, shifting the O / O<sub>2</sub> / O<sub>3</sub> equilibrium so that less UV is absorbed there and more reaches the surface, cooling the stratosphere as well.</p><p>The consequences run through every system the atmosphere's energy drives: climate, thermohaline circulation, the hydrological cycle, sea level and species distributions, plus UV damage to organisms. Predicting them is hard because the processes are linked - one change alters others - and because feedback can amplify (albedo loss, methane release) or damp (cloud cover, photosynthesis) the effect, sometimes past tipping points. Time lags from ocean heat capacity and the long residence times of CO<sub>2</sub> and CFCs, natural variability from solar output, volcanoes and orbital cycles, sparse historical data replaced by proxies, and models built on different assumptions all add uncertainty.</p><p>Some things are nevertheless well established: the physics of infrared absorption, the chemistry of ozone loss, and the success of the Montreal Protocol. What is uncertain is the scale and timing of regional impacts, which is exactly why the precautionary principle, not certainty, should guide action.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Humans release greenhouse gases like carbon dioxide from burning fossil fuels into the troposphere, which traps more heat and causes global warming. CFCs go into the stratosphere and destroy the ozone layer, so more UV reaches the Earth. It is hard to predict what will happen because the atmosphere is complicated and scientists do not know everything about it. There could be feedback effects that make it worse.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 gives one cause per layer; Level 3 lists several gases and processes and explains the mechanism (absorption and re-emission; catalytic destruction shifting an equilibrium).",
                  "'Difficulties of prediction' is answered in Level 3 with specific reasons (linked processes, feedback, time lags, natural variability, proxy data, models); Level 1 says 'complicated'.",
                  "Level 3 evaluates - separating what is certain from what is not - and links this to the precautionary principle."]},
    "HYD-13": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>A reservoir site is judged first on topography: a narrow exit from a large, deep basin lets a small, cheap dam hold a large volume. The bedrock must be impermeable so water is not lost and strong enough, without faults or seismic activity, to carry the dam. The catchment should be large with regular, high rainfall and low evaporation, and its land uses should not pose a pollution risk from industry or pesticides or cause the soil erosion that would silt the reservoir. Flooding forest or vegetation causes anaerobic decay and methane. Access for workers and materials, distance to the area of demand, and the value of the land lost - urban areas and nature reserves protected, farmland less so - complete a loss-benefit analysis that different countries weigh differently.</p><p>Supply can also be increased without new reservoirs: rainwater collection in cities, estuary barrages, desalination where freshwater is genuinely inadequate, newly found aquifers in North Africa and Kenya, inter-basin transfers as in China and from Wales to England, artificial recharge of aquifers with wet-season surplus, river-regulation reservoirs and afforestation to even out flow. Demand can be cut by low-water appliances, xeriscaping, drip irrigation, grey-water re-use, metering and repairing the leaks that lose about a fifth of UK supply, and treatment lets contaminated sources be re-used.</p><p>Reservoirs give large, reliable supplies and flood control but flood land and habitats and release methane; conservation is cheap and quick but limited by behaviour; transfers create conflicts between regions; desalination carries a high energy and carbon cost. As demand grows, the sustainable answer is conservation first, then a combination of sources matched to each region.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (4 marks)",
        "low": "<p>A reservoir needs a large catchment area with lots of rain, impermeable rock so the water does not drain away, and a narrow valley so the dam is small. It should be near the people who need the water and not flood important land. Other ways to get water include desalination, which is expensive, and collecting rainwater. Water can be conserved by fixing leaks, using water meters and having low-flush toilets. All of these help make water supplies more sustainable.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 lists correct site factors but without the reasons (why impermeable, why the valley shape matters, why land use in the catchment matters, methane from flooded vegetation).",
                  "Level 3 covers far more alternatives (aquifer recharge, river regulation, transfers, afforestation) and evaluates each; Level 2 evaluates only desalination.",
                  "Level 3 ends with a prioritised judgement; Level 2 ends with 'all of these help'."]},
    "REN-09": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Solar power's problems are intrinsic: it is intermittent with daily and seasonal cycles, cloud makes it unpredictable and scatters light so it cannot be focused, its low energy density demands large collector areas, the angle of incidence keeps changing, cleaning in deserts uses scarce water, manufacturing PV produces toxic wastes such as silicon tetrachloride and cadmium, and PV efficiency falls as cells heat up.</p><p>New technology addresses most of these. Evacuated-tube photothermal panels cut conduction and convection losses, and thermal stores of water, sand, concrete or molten salt hold heat for later; passive solar design, brise soleil and light tubes reduce demand; heat pumps deliver up to four times the energy used to run them. Multijunction PV cells absorb more wavelengths, moth-eye anti-reflective surfaces stop the 30% of light a smooth cell reflects, PV-thermal hybrids cool the cell and use the heat, transparent PV turns windows into generators, heliostats keep panels at the optimum angle and nanohydrophobic surfaces self-clean. Concentrating solar power with molten salt stored at 550 &deg;C runs steam turbines when electricity is wanted, directly overcoming intermittency, and grid-connected farms, rooftops that avoid land conflicts, batteries and pumped storage integrate the output.</p><p>Even so, no technology changes the fact that winter insolation at high latitudes is tiny, that CSP needs cloudless skies, or that manufacturing has impacts. Solar can dominate supply in sunny, low-latitude regions and contribute strongly elsewhere in summer, but overcoming its problems fully depends on storage and a diverse energy mix rather than on solar technology alone.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (2 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Solar power has problems because it does not work at night or when it is cloudy, and solar panels are not very efficient. New technologies can help. Better solar panels are more efficient. Solar power can be stored in batteries for use at night. Mirrors can concentrate the sunlight to make it hotter. So new technologies can overcome most of the problems with solar power.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 identifies two problems and three vague technologies; Level 3 names each technology and explains which problem it solves and how.",
                  "Level 3 acknowledges what technology cannot fix (high-latitude winters, cloud for CSP, manufacturing impacts) - the 'extent' in the question.",
                  "Numbers (30% reflection, 550 &deg;C, four-fold heat-pump output) show detailed knowledge applied to the argument."]},
    "FN-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Technology has repeatedly extended fossil-fuel supplies. Secondary and tertiary recovery raise the proportion of oil recovered from about 20% to 60%; directional drilling, subsea wells and ROVs open deep-water fields; fracking releases tight oil and gas; tar sands and oil shales, coal gasification and liquefaction convert lower-grade resources; and carbon capture and storage could reduce the CO<sub>2</sub> impact. Nuclear fission can be stretched even further: uranium can be adsorbed from seawater with polymers or recovered from phosphate mining and coal ash, molten-salt reactors run more safely, and plutonium breeder and thorium reactors multiply the energy obtainable from each tonne of fuel, with fusion a long-term prospect.</p><p>None of this makes the resources renewable. Discoveries of conventional oil have declined since the 1980s, unconventional sources cost more energy and money per unit and damage aquifers, use huge volumes of water, cause tremors and take land, and CCS is unproven at scale. Nuclear projects overrun their budgets, decommissioning and long-lived waste remain unsolved, public opposition and proliferation constrain expansion, low-grade uranium has a high embodied energy, and fusion is not commercial.</p><p>Sustainability means maintaining supply without unacceptable environmental, economic or social impact. New technology can extend supplies for decades and cut some impacts, which matters for closing the energy gap while renewables and conservation scale up, but it cannot make non-renewable resources sustainable indefinitely. Their proper role is as a transition and base-load source alongside renewables, not a permanent solution.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>New technologies mean fossil fuels can last longer. Fracking gets gas out of shale rock and directional drilling reaches oil under the sea. Carbon capture and storage can stop the carbon dioxide going into the atmosphere. For nuclear power, breeder reactors and thorium reactors can use fuel more efficiently and fusion might work in the future. However fossil fuels are still non-renewable so they will run out eventually, and fracking can pollute groundwater. Nuclear waste is still a problem. So new technologies help but they cannot make fossil fuels and nuclear power fully sustainable.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 reaches a sensible conclusion but supports it thinly; Level 3 gives specific evidence (20% to 60% recovery, declining discoveries, embodied energy of low-grade uranium).",
                  "Level 3 sets out a criterion for sustainability and judges against it - the hallmark of 'evaluate'.",
                  "Level 3 places the technologies in context (transition and base load alongside renewables) rather than treating them in isolation."]},
    "MIN-10": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Lasky's principle means that as grade falls, the quantity of metal available rises enormously, so depletion of high-grade deposits does not mean running out - it means the cut-off ore grade must fall, which depends on price, technology and energy costs. Better exploration finds what remains: high-resolution multispectral and infrared satellite sensors, gravimetry, magnetometry, seismic and resistivity surveys and portable field analysers narrow the search so expensive trial drilling is targeted. Mechanisation allows deeper underground mines and larger open-cast pits, and previously inaccessible deposits - polymetallic nodules and deep-sea sediments, and Antarctica if its protection were ever lifted - become possible, with serious environmental and legal problems.</p><p>The most sustainable route to low-grade ores is low-energy extraction: bioleaching with acidophilic bacteria and fungi, phytomining, iron displacement of copper, bacterial adsorption of rare earths and polymer adsorption from seawater all avoid the energy of crushing and smelting vast tonnages. Equally important is extending the use of metal already mined: recycling, Cradle-to-Cradle design so products can be disassembled, longer product lifetimes and substitution of scarce metals - the circular economy approach.</p><p>Each strategy has costs: deep-sea mining and Antarctica risk unique habitats, deeper mines need more energy, recycling is limited by dispersal and mixed alloys. The strategies that mimic natural low-temperature processes and keep metals in use are the most sustainable, and exploration and new mining should be the last resort rather than the first.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>When high grade ores run out we can mine lower grade ores instead, but this uses more energy. Better technology like satellites can find new deposits. We could mine the deep sea or Antarctica. Bacteria can be used to extract metals from low grade ore. Recycling metals means we do not need to mine as much. These strategies will help secure supplies of metals in the future.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 names most strategy types but Level 3 explains each (what the surveys measure, why low-temperature extraction matters, what Lasky's principle implies).",
                  "Level 3 weighs environmental and energy costs and ranks the strategies; Level 1 offers no evaluation.",
                  "Framing the answer with the concept of cut-off ore grade shows understanding rather than recall."]},
    "NOISE-06": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Because the decibel scale is logarithmic, a 10 dB cut is a tenfold reduction in intensity and 3 dB halves it, so modest-sounding design changes matter. Aircraft engines have been made quieter by high-bypass-ratio turbofans, chevron nozzles, hush kits and acoustic liners, with fairings and composite airframes reducing airframe noise. Operation can be changed too: steeper take-off angles and flight paths that avoid housing, constant-descent approaches, restricted reverse thrust, night curfews, Chapter noise limits enforced by fines and quota counts. Airport location and layout - away from population centres, engine-test areas and taxiways kept from housing, acoustic walls and baffle mounds, land-use planning and free insulation for residents - deal with the noise that remains.</p><p>Railways use track polishing, sound-absorbing ballast and suspension, pantograph fairings, wheel lubrication on curves and composite brakes. Roads use porous or rubber-crumb asphalt, acoustic fences and embankments, tree planting, engine insulation and aerodynamics, traffic routing and double glazing. Industry fits silencers to compressors, presses rather than stamps, uses nylon rollers, drills instead of pile-driving, surrounds piling with bubble curtains to protect cetaceans, times blasting, hangs acoustic mats and operates remotely, with ear protection as a last line. Domestic appliances use acoustic absorbers and volume limiters.</p><p>Design changes at source are the most effective because they reduce noise for everyone; operational limits depend on enforcement; location works only for new developments, and airports are still sited near cities for convenience. Legislation and monitoring of noise contours (57 dB L<sub>eq</sub>) are what turn these options into actual reductions.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Noise pollution can be reduced in different ways. Aircraft can be designed with quieter engines and can fly at night less often. Airports can be built away from cities. Roads can have sound barriers next to them and quieter road surfaces. Trains can have their tracks polished and use better brakes. Factories can use quieter machines and workers can wear ear defenders. Noise can also be reduced by planting trees. These methods reduce the noise that reaches people.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 gives correct but generic examples ('quieter engines'); Level 3 names the design features (high bypass ratio, chevron nozzles, acoustic liners) the question's 'named examples' requires.",
                  "Level 3 organises by design / operation / location as the question asks; Level 2 mixes them.",
                  "The decibel-scale point and the closing evaluation of which approaches work best lift Level 3 into the top band."]},
    "NUT-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Organic wastes - sewage, manure, silage effluent, paper-mill and food-processing effluent - are food for aerobic bacteria, whose respiration strips dissolved oxygen from the water so fish and invertebrates suffocate and bacterial growths smother the bed; sewage also carries pathogens such as cholera and typhoid. Inorganic nutrients act differently: nitrate leached from fertiliser and manure and phosphate from sewage, detergents and eroded soil cause cultural eutrophication, in which algal blooms shade macrophytes, cyanobacteria release toxins and night-time respiration and decay deoxygenate the water. Nitrate in drinking water can cause methaemoglobinaemia and may form carcinogenic nitrosamines.</p><p>Point sources are controlled by sewage treatment: screening and grit removal, primary sedimentation removing most organic solids, aeration with activated sludge or trickling filters, and anaerobic digestion of sludge yielding biogas. Nutrients need tertiary treatment - phosphate stripping with iron(III) sulfate, denitrifying filter beds, micro-strainers and UV. Diffuse agricultural sources are managed by Nitrate Vulnerable Zones, limits on the timing and amount of fertiliser, low-solubility fertilisers, legumes, manure management and buffer strips.</p><p>Sewage treatment has virtually eliminated river deoxygenation and waterborne disease in developed countries, but storm overflows on combined sewers still discharge untreated sewage, and tertiary treatment is expensive so it is used only where eutrophication is a risk. Diffuse nitrate from farmland is much harder to control because it comes from thousands of sources and depends on farmers' behaviour and the weather. Control of point sources has therefore been far more successful than control of diffuse ones.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Sewage in rivers uses up the oxygen because bacteria break it down, so fish die. Fertilisers wash into rivers and cause eutrophication, where algae grow and block the light and then the water runs out of oxygen. Sewage is treated in sewage works to clean it before it goes into rivers. Farmers should use less fertiliser. Sewage treatment works well but fertiliser is still a problem.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 has the outline of both mechanisms but Level 3 explains them precisely (aerobic respiration, shading, night-time respiration, pathogens, health effects) and names the pollutant sources.",
                  "Level 3 describes the stages of sewage treatment and the specific nutrient controls; Level 1 says 'sewage works' and 'less fertiliser'.",
                  "Level 3's evaluation gives reasons (storm overflows, cost of tertiary treatment, diffuse sources) for its point-vs-diffuse judgement; Level 1 asserts it."]},
    "PEST-04": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Neonicotinoids are neurotoxins related to nicotine that block the neurotransmitter acetylcholine. They are highly toxic to insects but of low toxicity to vertebrates because they cannot cross the vertebrate blood-brain barrier - the specificity that made them popular. Bees, however, are insects: even sub-lethal doses impair navigation, foraging and immunity, and this has been linked to colony decline. Because the compounds are water-soluble and moderately persistent they remain in the soil, are absorbed by roots and translocated through the plant (systemic action), so they appear in pollen and nectar that bees collect; persistence may also allow build-up in soils and aquifers, although sunlight and soil microbes break them down. Some fungicides act synergistically with them, increasing toxicity to bees.</p><p>Exposure is greatest when crops are sprayed or when dust from coated seeds is blown by wind, so impacts are reduced by using seed coatings instead of sprays, spraying only when crops are not in flower or at night when bees are not foraging, and applying on still days to limit drift. The EU has banned outdoor use on flowering crops, and integrated control with cultural and biological methods reduces the need for insecticides altogether.</p><p>These measures reduce bee exposure, but the alternatives farmers turn to, such as pyrethroids, also harm pollinators, and restrictions can reduce yields, so the trade-off between crop protection and pollination services remains.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (4 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Neonicotinoids are insecticides that kill insects by affecting their nervous system. They are systemic so they get into the whole plant including the pollen, and bees pick them up when they collect pollen and nectar. This harms bees and can cause colonies to die. To reduce the impact, farmers can spray when crops are not flowering, and some neonicotinoids have been banned in the EU. Other methods of pest control could be used instead.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 links the systemic property to bee exposure correctly but omits the other properties (solubility, persistence, specificity via the blood-brain barrier, synergism) that the question's 'properties' demands.",
                  "Level 3 explains the sub-lethal effects (navigation, foraging, immunity) rather than 'harms bees'.",
                  "Level 3 includes the trade-off evaluation (alternatives also harm pollinators, yield costs) that the mark scheme rewards."]},
    "PPRO-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>A pollutant's properties largely determine where it goes and how much harm it does, so they can be used both to predict its behaviour and to choose a control. State of matter and density govern dispersal: solids deposit near the source, gases spread widely, and dense gases such as hydrogen cyanide at Bhopal hug the ground. Persistence decides whether dilution is enough: non-persistent wastes like sewage can be discharged where they break down, but organochlorines, CFCs, heavy metals and long-lived radioisotopes travel far and accumulate, so they need containment, bans or restrictions instead. Toxicity, specificity and mode of action, quantified by LD50 and used to set acceptable doses, identify which species are at risk. Solubility explains why nitrate leaches and is controlled by nitrate vulnerable zones, while liposolubility explains why DDT and methyl mercury bioaccumulate and biomagnify along food chains. Reactive pollutants may degrade quickly or form secondary pollutants - ozone, acid rain, PANs - so controlling the primary emissions with catalytic converters or flue-gas desulfurisation prevents the secondary ones. Adsorption on clay immobilises metals and caesium and is exploited deliberately in activated-carbon and polymer filters. Mutagens and carcinogens have no safe dose, so exposure is kept As Low As Reasonably Achievable, and synergism between ozone and sulfur dioxide, or smoke and sulfur dioxide, means pollutants must be assessed together.</p><p>Properties interact with the environment - inversions, currents, pH, oxygen - which is why Critical Pathway Analysis combines both to predict where pollutants concentrate and to focus monitoring. The greatest value of this understanding is preventive: the properties of a new chemical can be predicted from similar ones under the precautionary principle, allowing control before release rather than clean-up afterwards.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (2 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Pollutants have properties like toxicity, persistence and solubility. Toxic pollutants are dangerous. Persistent pollutants like DDT last a long time in the environment and build up in food chains. Soluble pollutants dissolve in water and get into rivers. Knowing these properties helps scientists to predict what pollutants will do and to control them, for example by banning DDT.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 defines three properties in isolation; Level 3 pairs every property with a prediction <i>and</i> a control strategy, which is what the question asks.",
                  "Level 3 uses a wide range of accurate examples (Bhopal, nitrate zones, methyl mercury, PANs, caesium adsorption); Level 1 uses DDT twice.",
                  "The final paragraph links properties to environmental conditions (CPA) and to the precautionary principle - synoptic evaluation that distinguishes the top band."]},
    "RAD-06": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Ionising radiation is used because it does things nothing else can: nuclear fission generates low-carbon electricity from fuel of extraordinary energy density; medicine relies on X-rays and CT scans, radiotherapy, tracers and gamma sterilisation of equipment; industry uses thickness gauges, cross-links polymers, tests turbine blades and explores for oil; and agriculture uses irradiation to preserve food and sterilise insect pests. The risks arise because radiation produces free radicals that damage DNA, causing mutation and cancer, with acute and chronic, somatic and gonadic effects; contamination is worse than exposure because the source stays with the person; and activation products, long-lived wastes, accidents at Chernobyl and Fukushima and weapons fallout add environmental risks.</p><p>Risk depends on the type of radiation - alpha is barely penetrating but has a weighting factor of 20 - on half-life, on dose in grays and sieverts and on the route of exposure. Weighing risks against benefits is difficult because cancers appear years later with many other possible causes, exposure data are poor, the people who benefit are often not those at risk, and benefits such as avoided surgery are hard to quantify.</p><p>Control follows ALARA and BATNEEC: closed sources, remote handling, shielding, distance under the inverse-square law, limiting time, protective clothing, decontamination and potassium iodide against iodine-131. Workers wear dosemeters and film badges and pass contamination monitors; discharges are managed by Critical Pathway Analysis and Critical Group Monitoring; wastes are vitrified, encapsulated or stored at licensed sites. With these controls the benefits generally outweigh the risks - natural radon supplies about half the average dose and the nuclear industry under one per cent - although public perception of risk remains far higher than the measured risk.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Ionising radiation is used in nuclear power stations to make electricity, in hospitals for X-rays and treating cancer, and in industry. The risks are that radiation damages DNA and causes cancer, and accidents like Chernobyl release radiation into the environment. Nuclear waste stays dangerous for a long time. Workers are protected by shielding, keeping their distance and wearing film badges to measure their dose. The public is protected by monitoring around nuclear sites. Overall the benefits are worth the risks if it is controlled properly.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 covers uses, risks, controls and a judgement, but each briefly; Level 3 adds the factors that determine risk (type, half-life, dose units, route) and why risk-benefit analysis is difficult.",
                  "Level 3 names the principles (ALARA, BATNEEC, inverse square law, CPA / CGM) rather than describing practice loosely.",
                  "The judgement in Level 3 is supported with evidence (radon vs nuclear industry doses); Level 2's is an opinion."]},
    "REN-08": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>What renewables can contribute depends on their properties - intermittency, predictability, energy density, storage, applicability, location, impacts, technological maturity and cost - set against the UK's resources. Wind is the largest: shallow seas give a huge offshore resource, costs have fallen and direct-drive and vertical-axis designs keep improving, but it is intermittent, needs storage and grid connections and provokes land-use conflicts onshore. Solar PV costs are falling rapidly and roof space is plentiful, but winter insolation is low and concentrating solar is unsuitable; photothermal panels and heat pumps are better for heating. Most large HEP sites are used, though low-head, helical and micro-hydro extend it and pumped storage is vital for balancing. The Severn and Mersey have large, predictable tidal ranges, but barrages are costly and damaging while lagoons and in-stream turbines have lower impact; the long Atlantic fetch gives wave energy that the technology cannot yet harness reliably. Biofuels from wastes and anaerobic digestion are useful, but energy crops compete with food and habitat, and geothermal is limited to low-temperature schemes.</p><p>The system matters as much as the sources: storage in pumped HEP, batteries, hydrogen and power-to-gas, vehicle-to-grid, grid management, a diverse mix that smooths supply, and conservation to reduce demand. With these, renewables could supply most of the UK's electricity. Heat and transport are harder, because they need high temperatures or liquid fuels, so electrification and hydrogen would be required. The potential is large, but realising it needs sustained investment and changes in how energy is used.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>The UK could use lots of renewable energy. It is windy so wind turbines could be built, especially offshore. Solar panels can be put on roofs but it is not very sunny in winter. Tidal power could be used in the Severn estuary because it has big tides. Hydroelectric power can be used in Scotland. Renewables are intermittent so batteries would be needed. Renewables could provide a lot of the UK's energy in the future.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 mentions the main resources with one fact each; Level 3 assesses each against its properties, constraints and the state of the technology.",
                  "Level 3 treats storage, grid and demand as a system and distinguishes electricity from heat and transport - essential for judging 'future energy supplies'.",
                  "The conclusion in Level 3 is qualified (most electricity; heat and transport harder; needs investment); Level 1's is unqualified."]},
    "AGI-04": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Sustainability matters because the population may reach 12 billion, land has been degraded since farming began in the fertile crescent, and current yields depend on artificial fertilisers, pesticides and irrigation that draws down groundwater, with little new land to cultivate. Pest control can move from chemicals to cultural methods - weeding, mulching, rotation, barrier and companion crops, habitats for predators - biological control, sterile males and pheromones, resistant varieties and integrated control, with fewer antibiotics in livestock.</p><p>Nutrients can come from natural processes: legumes fixing nitrogen, recycling manure and compost, rotation, permaculture and conserving soil biota and mycorrhizae, reducing manufactured nitrate and the non-renewable rock phosphate it accompanies. Energy use falls with low tillage, less machinery, renewable power, low food miles and lower methane from cattle diets and drought-tolerant rice. Water is conserved with low water-use crops, drip irrigation, reservoirs and aquifer recharge and by avoiding saline water; soil by maintaining organic matter and controlling erosion. Retaining hedgerows, ditches, ponds and woodland, conserving crop wild relatives in seed banks and agri-environment schemes protect the biodiversity that supplies pollination and pest control, while Fairtrade, local food and consumer choices address the social side.</p><p>The tension is between yield and sustainability: some strategies cost more or yield less in the short term and need policy support to be adopted. But since the unsustainable inputs themselves depend on finite fossil fuels and phosphate, the strategies that use natural processes are the only ones that can feed a larger population indefinitely.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (4 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Agriculture can be made more sustainable by using less pesticides and using biological control instead, using manure instead of artificial fertilisers, growing legumes which add nitrogen to the soil, and using crop rotation. Drip irrigation saves water. Farmers can keep hedgerows to provide habitats for wildlife. Organic farming uses these methods. These strategies reduce pollution and keep the soil healthy so food can be produced in the future.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 lists valid strategies but does not explain why they are needed (population growth, land degradation, dependence on finite inputs) or group them by the problem they solve.",
                  "Level 3 covers energy, soil, biodiversity and social strategies as well as pests, nutrients and water.",
                  "Level 3 discusses the yield-sustainability tension and the need for policy support; Level 2 has no evaluation."]},
    "AQUA-05": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Wild catches have not increased since about 1990 because most stocks are fully or over-exploited, while aquaculture has grown rapidly and now approaches the wild catch, so the question is how far it can take over. Its advantages are real: limiting factors, breeding, gender and disease can be controlled, yields per area are high, and fish have low food conversion ratios because they need not support their weight or keep warm; herbivores such as tilapia and carp and filter-feeding molluscs need no fishmeal at all.</p><p>Its limits are equally real. The carnivorous species consumers prefer - salmon, shrimp - are fed fishmeal from wild forage fish, so their farming still depends on fishing and depletes species such as sandeels on which puffins rely; some species, such as bluefin tuna, will not breed in captivity. Farms destroy mangroves, release organic waste, pesticides and antibiotics that breed resistance, spread lice, allow escapes that contaminate wild gene pools and provoke predator control, though siting, closed systems, polyculture and vaccines can reduce these. Meanwhile fishing itself can be sustainable if quotas based on good stock data, gear design, size and effort limits and no-take zones are enforced.</p><p>Aquaculture can therefore supply most of the growth in demand, especially through herbivorous fish and shellfish, and can relieve pressure on wild stocks, but it cannot fully replace fishing while carnivorous species depend on wild fishmeal and some species cannot be farmed. Both must be managed sustainably; neither is a substitute for the other.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (2 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Aquaculture is farming fish instead of catching them. Fish farms can produce a lot of fish and they do not run out like wild fish do. But fish farms cause pollution and diseases can spread to wild fish. Salmon farms feed the salmon on fish which have to be caught. So aquaculture can replace some fishing but not all of it.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 reaches a reasonable conclusion but supports it with two sentences; Level 3 gives the context (flat wild catch since 1990), the advantages in detail and the impacts with ways to reduce them.",
                  "Level 3 distinguishes between species types (herbivores and molluscs vs carnivores) - the key to 'to what extent'.",
                  "Level 3 also considers whether fishing can be made sustainable, which the comparison requires."]},
    "CBR-09": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>In-situ conservation keeps a species in its natural habitat with the whole community it depends on - food species, pollinators, predators and the abiotic conditions it is adapted to. It is delivered through protected areas such as SSSIs, NNRs, SACs, SPAs, Ramsar sites and Marine Conservation Zones, land ownership by conservation bodies, agri-environment schemes and habitat creation and management. Its weaknesses are that external threats such as pollution and climate change cross reserve boundaries, enforcement and cost, conflicts with other land uses, and the fact that many populations are already small and fragmented.</p><p>Ex-situ captive breeding is used when in-situ protection is impossible or insufficient. It is hard: animals need enough space, the right food, social groupings and breeding triggers, and small founder populations risk inbreeding and hybridisation, which studbooks, artificial insemination and embryo transfer help to manage. It is expensive, works for few species, and captive animals may lose wild behaviour; release succeeds only where suitable habitat exists and with soft release and post-release monitoring.</p><p>The two are not alternatives. Ex-situ methods are a temporary safeguard that can rescue a species from extinction, as with the Arabian oryx, but they only succeed if there is protected habitat to return to. In-situ conservation is therefore fundamental and ex-situ a support for it; the relative importance depends on how close to extinction the species is.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>In-situ conservation means protecting species in their natural habitat, for example in nature reserves and national parks. This is good because the species stays in the wild with the other species it needs. Ex-situ conservation means captive breeding in zoos and then releasing the animals back into the wild. This can save species that are nearly extinct but it is expensive and animals may not survive when released. Both methods are important and are often used together.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 defines both approaches correctly but Level 3 gives specific mechanisms (types of protected area, breeding difficulties, release methods) and named examples.",
                  "Level 3 weighs the limitations of each approach explicitly; Level 2 gives one limitation for ex-situ only.",
                  "'Both are important' is not a judgement about <i>relative</i> importance; Level 3 explains why in-situ is fundamental and when ex-situ matters most."]},
    "AGI-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Present agriculture relies on inputs that cannot last: chemical pesticides to which pests evolve resistance and which harm non-target species, rock phosphate that is non-renewable, nitrate fertiliser made with fossil-fuel energy, and irrigation that over-draws rivers and groundwater and salinises soil, while ploughing loses soil organic matter and livestock, rice and machinery release greenhouse gases. Each can be replaced by strategies based on ecological principles. Pests are controlled culturally by weeding, mulching, rotation, barrier crops and culling, by biological control and predator habitats, by polyculture and companion crops, and by integrated control that uses chemicals only as a last resort. Nutrients come from recycled organic matter, rotation with legumes and conserved soil biota, so artificial fertiliser, especially nitrate, can be reduced. Water is saved with low water-use crops, reservoirs and aquifer recharge and drip irrigation; soil carbon is kept by low tillage and less machinery, with renewable energy for what remains; methane falls with rice varieties that allow earlier drainage and with ground, carbohydrate-rich cattle feed. Habitats of crop wild relatives, seed banks, hedgerows, ditches, ponds and woodland and agri-environment schemes protect the biodiversity that supplies these services.</p><p>Food demand will rise with a population that may reach 12 billion, there is little new land, and imports can reduce food availability where it is grown, so yield cannot be ignored. Some strategies cost more or need farmers to accept new techniques, and past land degradation from the fertile crescent to the American mid-west shows what happens when they are not adopted. No single strategy suffices; a combination of ecological principles with new technology is the only route to feeding more people without exhausting the resources food depends on.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (2 marks)",
        "low": "<p>To make agriculture sustainable farmers should use fewer chemicals. Organic farming does not use pesticides or artificial fertilisers. Crop rotation and manure keep the soil fertile. Farmers should not waste water. Hedges should be kept for wildlife. This would make farming more sustainable but it might produce less food.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 gives general advice; Level 3 identifies each unsustainable feature and matches it with specific strategies, showing understanding of why they work.",
                  "Level 3's evaluation considers population growth, land limits, cost, adoption and historical evidence; Level 1's is a single clause.",
                  "Note that Level 3 avoids defining organic farming as 'not using chemicals' - the textbook stresses it is the positive use of natural processes."]},
    "FISH-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Fishing's impacts go beyond depleting target species: k-selected species that breed late and produce few young collapse and recover slowly; by-catch of immature target fish, over-quota fish and non-commercial species is thrown back dead, with drift nets killing whales, dolphins and turtles, longlines drowning albatrosses and pair trawls porpoises; lost gear keeps ghost fishing; demersal trawls destroy sea fans, corals and seagrass; and removing any species alters its competitors, prey and predators.</p><p>Catch quotas limit the weight landed and work well for single-species pelagic shoals where fishing stops when the quota is reached, but in mixed demersal fisheries over-quota fish are discarded dead, so the quota does not protect them. Gear design tackles by-catch directly: mesh sizes and square-mesh escape panels let small fish out because, unlike diagonal mesh, they do not close under drag; turtle exclusion devices, acoustic pingers for dolphins, curved hooks, decoys, sinkers and night setting protect turtles, cetaceans and albatrosses; biodegradable and radio-tracked traps limit ghost fishing. Restricting effort - boat size, engine power, days at sea, decommissioning - reduces total pressure, and banning drift nets or demersal trawling on sensitive seabeds such as Rockall Bank protects habitats.</p><p>Effectiveness depends on enforcement at sea, which is difficult, on reliable stock data for quotas, on the species and fishery type, and on international cooperation because fish migrate; some measures cut catches or raise costs, and as local stocks fall fleets simply move further away. Gear modifications are the most effective per unit cost because they reduce damage without reducing legal catch; quotas and effort limits only work where they are enforced.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Fishing damages the environment by overfishing, catching by-catch such as dolphins and damaging the seabed with trawling. Quotas limit how many fish can be caught so stocks can recover. Bigger mesh sizes let small fish escape so they can grow and breed. Turtle exclusion devices let turtles escape from nets. Some fishing methods can be banned in sensitive areas. No-take zones let fish breed. These methods help but they are hard to enforce at sea and some fishermen ignore them.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 covers impacts and methods soundly but without the mechanisms (why square mesh works, why quotas fail in mixed fisheries).",
                  "Level 3 evaluates each method on its own terms and then compares them; Level 2's evaluation is a single general sentence.",
                  "Named examples (Rockall Bank, albatross measures, ghost-fishing controls) give Level 3 the range the top band expects."]},
    "HAB-14": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Tropical coral reefs cover about one per cent of the oceans yet support over a quarter of marine species, a diversity built on the symbiosis between polyps and zooxanthellae that recycles nutrients in nutrient-poor water. They matter to people for fisheries of fish, lobsters and conch; for medicines such as AZT, Ara-C, dolastatin, secosteroids, bryostatin and trabectedin discovered in reef organisms; as a carbon sink, since every carbonate unit in coral skeleton absorbs a molecule of carbon dioxide; for dissipating wave energy that would erode low-lying islands; and for tourism income.</p><p>They are also unusually vulnerable because they need narrow abiotic conditions. Anchors, divers, litter and coral extraction kill polyps; ornaments are collected; sediment from rivers and coastal development smothers them; oil, sewage and nutrients cause algal shading; ocean acidification hinders skeleton formation; warming causes bleaching; overfishing of giant tritons lets crown-of-thorns starfish destroy reefs; introduced lionfish eat native fish; and loss of mangroves and seagrass removes the nurseries and sediment traps reefs depend on.</p><p>Marine protected areas such as the Great Barrier Reef, Belize and Chagos combine mooring buoys, diver education, no-take zones that raise catches outside them, sustainable tourism and protection of linked habitats, while fishing controls set closed seasons, minimum and maximum sizes and ban dynamite, trawling and small mesh. These work well against local threats where they are enforced, and no-take zones have measurable benefits, but they cannot touch warming and acidification, which need international action, enforcement in remote areas is weak, and restrictions conflict with local livelihoods. Local conservation buys time; only global climate action can secure the reefs.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Coral reefs are important because lots of fish live there and people catch them for food. Tourists visit reefs to dive and this brings money. Reefs also protect the coast from waves. Reefs are threatened by climate change which causes bleaching, and by pollution and damage from boats. They are conserved by making marine protected areas where fishing is banned. This helps but climate change is still a problem.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 has the right headings but Level 3 explains each (why reefs are diverse, why they are a carbon sink, how each threat acts).",
                  "Level 3 uses named reserves and specific management measures; Level 1 gives one generic measure.",
                  "Level 3's evaluation separates local from global threats and reaches a nuanced judgement; Level 1 states that climate change is 'still a problem'."]},
    "HAB-05": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Protected areas are the core of rainforest conservation. Korup National Park in Cameroon protects 1260 km<sup>2</sup> with about 600 tree species, including the endemic <i>Ancistrocladus korupensis</i> whose alkaloids have anti-HIV and antimalarial activity; Brazil's Tumucumaque National Park covers 39 000 km<sup>2</sup> and adjoins a reserve in French Guiana; the Rio Bravo area in Belize is funded by charities, companies, ecotourism and selective logging of high-value mahogany that does not damage the forest long term. Debt-for-nature swaps have cancelled Guatemalan and Brazilian debt in return for protection, CITES listing restricts trade in Honduras mahogany, the ITTO sets sustainable timber aims, ecotourism and community employment give local people an income from standing forest, and carbon sequestration payments are emerging.</p><p>Where they are enforced, protected areas work, but they face illegal logging, mining, poaching, road building and conflict, and ecotourism itself has damaged sites in Costa Rica and Malaysia. The deeper problem is that the drivers of deforestation - population growth and international demand for beef, soya, palm oil, timber and minerals - originate outside the forest, and climate change threatens even well-protected areas; the ITTO's aims are largely unmet and overall loss continues.</p><p>The most effective schemes are those that combine large protected areas with economic alternatives for local people, such as Rio Bravo, but they can only slow the loss unless demand in importing countries also falls.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (4 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Rainforests are protected by national parks where logging is banned, for example in Brazil. Debt-for-nature swaps mean a country's debt is cancelled if it protects its forest. Ecotourism brings money from tourists so the forest is worth more standing than cut down. Sustainable logging only takes some trees. These efforts have saved some areas but deforestation is still happening because of illegal logging and farming, so they have only been partly effective.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 covers the main types of effort and a fair conclusion, but the question says 'describe' - Level 3 gives named examples with figures (Korup, Tumucumaque, Rio Bravo) as the mark scheme expects.",
                  "Level 3 explains <i>why</i> effectiveness is limited (drivers originate outside the forest, ITTO aims unmet, climate change) rather than just noting continued loss.",
                  "The judgement in Level 3 identifies what works best and what would be needed; Level 2 stops at 'partly effective'."]},
    "BIOV-04": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Human society depends on other species first as resources: wood, fibres such as cotton, wool and silk, oils, fuels and, above all, food, with wild species such as the morama bean, perennial cereals, eland and bison offering new crops and livestock better adapted to local conditions. Biomimetics copies adaptations into vehicle design, architecture, structures, adhesives, materials and ultrasound; medicines such as taxol, quinine and AZT came from plants and animals, and physiological research on marsupials, hippos and sponges continues to yield medical insights. Wild species are pest-control agents - ladybirds, <i>Encarsia</i>, <i>Cactoblastis</i>, <i>Bacillus thuringiensis</i> - and crop wild relatives in Vavilov centres hold the genes future crops will need.</p><p>Less visibly, other species provide the services that keep the planet habitable: regulating atmospheric composition, driving the hydrological and biogeochemical cycles, maintaining soil, pollinating crops, dispersing seeds and providing habitat. Most species have never been investigated, so their value is unknown, and there are ethical, aesthetic and recreational reasons for conservation that owe nothing to usefulness.</p><p>Which arguments persuade decision-makers? Economic valuation of resources and services speaks the language of policy and has been most effective, but it undervalues the services that go unnoticed because they operate in dynamic equilibrium and it can never capture the value of species not yet studied. The strongest case combines the precautionary logic of unknown value with the intrinsic argument: conserving biodiversity protects both what we know we need and what we cannot yet know.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Biodiversity is important for humans in many ways. We get food, wood and cotton from plants and animals. Many medicines come from plants. Bees pollinate our crops so we need to conserve them. Animals and plants are also nice to look at and people like to visit nature. We should conserve biodiversity because we might need species in the future.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 names categories without examples; Level 3 gives specific, accurate examples in every category.",
                  "Level 3 includes ecosystem services, genetic resources and pest control that Level 1 omits.",
                  "'Discuss' at the top level means weighing the arguments against each other - Level 3 evaluates which arguments persuade and why; Level 1 does not."]},
    "LP-07": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (8 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Succession is the sequence by which pioneers colonise bare ground, modify the abiotic conditions and are replaced by later seres until a climax community controlled by climate is reached; where grazing, mowing, burning or coppicing halts it, a plagioclimax forms. This tells a conservation manager whether to intervene: chalk grassland, heathland and coppiced woodland are plagioclimaxes that disappear without continued management, whereas a climax habitat is best left alone, and habitat creation must plan the stages, control scrub and manage secondary succession.</p><p>Population dynamics - birth and death rates, density-dependent and independent factors, carrying capacity, sigmoid growth, predator-prey cycles and r- and k-selection - inform decisions of a different kind. Maximum sustainable yield sets harvest levels in fisheries, whaling and forestry; k-selected species that breed late and slowly need strict protection and recover slowly; culling may be justified where predators are absent, as with Scottish red deer without wolves, or where introduced species threaten natives, while the reintroduction of wolves to Yellowstone shows how restoring a predator changes vegetation through the food web. Monitoring population size, distribution, survival and age structure measures success, and knowledge of adaptation and ranges of tolerance guides how abiotic conditions are managed.</p><p>The limits are uncertainty: natural fluctuations, unknown carrying capacities and the difficulty of predicting how communities respond. Ecological understanding cannot remove that uncertainty, but without it conservation decisions - to manage or not, to cull or protect, how much to harvest - are guesses.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 2 (5 marks)",
        "low": "<p>Succession is when a habitat changes over time from pioneer species to a climax community. Conservationists need to understand this because some habitats like heathland will turn into woodland if they are not managed by grazing or burning. Population dynamics is about how populations grow and are limited by factors like food and predators. This helps conservation because you can work out how many animals can be taken without the population declining, and when a population needs protecting. Monitoring populations shows if conservation is working.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 2 explains both concepts correctly and links them to decisions, but generally; Level 3 uses the technical vocabulary (seres, plagioclimax, k-selection, MSY) with named cases (Yellowstone wolves, Scottish deer, chalk grassland).",
                  "Level 3 covers more applications (habitat creation, culling introduced species, adaptation and tolerance) and evaluates the uncertainties.",
                  "Structure: Level 3 gives each concept its own paragraph with the decisions it informs, then a conclusion."]},
    "SUS-06": {
        "top_level": "Level 3 (9 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Taking water, waste and pollution control: water supply is already close to circular, because the hydrological cycle returns what is used. Catchment management, conservation, metering, grey-water re-use, re-use of treated effluent, aquifer recharge and sustainable urban drainage close the loop further, with the main limit in arid regions where abstraction exceeds recharge. Waste management can apply the principles almost completely: repair, re-use and refurbishment extend product life, recycling returns materials, composting and anaerobic digestion return nutrients and yield biogas, residual waste supplies energy and landfill is minimised, driven by EU targets on packaging, batteries and WEEE; the limits are mixed materials that cannot be separated and the energy cost of collection.</p><p>Pollution control changes character under circular principles: instead of treating wastes after production - flue-gas desulfurisation, dispersants - the aim becomes non-release, through fuel desulfurisation, fuel cells and electric vehicles, and industrial symbiosis, as at Kalundborg where one plant's waste heat, gypsum, fly ash and yeast become neighbours' raw materials. Land use can support ecosystems with green infrastructure, gardens, permeable surfaces and habitat in redeveloped docks.</p><p>Applicability is high for water and waste because natural cycles already exist to copy; it is partial for pollution control because many processes still depend on combustion, and it needs policy support - targets, producer responsibility, planning - to overcome the economics of linear systems. The principles can be applied widely, but not evenly.</p>",
        "low_level": "Level 1 (3 marks)",
        "low": "<p>The circular economy means reusing and recycling things instead of throwing them away. For water this means saving water and reusing grey water. For waste management it means recycling more and putting less in landfill. For pollution control it means not producing pollution in the first place. The circular economy can be applied to all of these to make them more sustainable.</p>",
        "notes": ["Level 1 gives one idea per area; Level 3 gives several specific measures for each and names the limit in each case.",
                  "Level 3 uses a case study (Kalundborg) and policy examples (EU targets) to show application, not just principle.",
                  "'Evaluate the extent' - Level 3 compares applicability across the three areas and explains the differences; Level 1 says it applies to all."]},
}

# Two full essays (Question 11) - one per paper
ESSAY_EXEMPLARS = {
    "ESS-P1-CONTROL": {
        "top_level": "Level 5 (22 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Pollutants cause damage in proportion to how much reaches sensitive organisms and how harmful it is when it gets there. Both depend on properties, so knowing a pollutant's properties lets us predict where it will go and which control will work.</p><p>State of matter and density decide dispersal. Solid particulates settle near the source unless they are very small: PM10 and smaller stay suspended, travel far and penetrate lungs, so smoke is controlled at the point of release with electrostatic precipitators, cyclones and bag filters, and smokeless zones prevent its production. Gases disperse widely, and dense gases such as the hydrogen cyanide released at Bhopal hug the ground, so location and emission height matter. Persistence decides whether dilution is acceptable. Sewage and other biodegradable wastes break down, so treatment plus dilution in a river or the sea is sufficient; organochlorine pesticides, CFCs, heavy metals and long-lived radioisotopes are not broken down, so they travel, accumulate and must be contained, banned or replaced - DDT and CFCs were both controlled by substitution once their persistence was understood.</p><p>Solubility governs the pathway. Water-soluble nitrate leaches into rivers and aquifers, so it is controlled by nitrate vulnerable zones, fertiliser timing and buffer strips; liposoluble pollutants such as DDT and methyl mercury pass into fat and are biomagnified along food chains, as at Minamata, so even low concentrations in water are dangerous and the only effective control is to stop release. Adsorption on clay immobilises caesium and metals, which is why radioactive discharges are monitored in sediments and why activated carbon and polymer adsorbents are used deliberately in effluent treatment.</p><p>Toxicity, specificity and mode of action set acceptable doses and identify species at risk. Neonicotinoids are toxic to insects but not vertebrates because they cannot cross the vertebrate blood-brain barrier, which explains both their popularity and their effect on bees; pyrethroids replaced organochlorines because they are less persistent. For mutagens and carcinogens such as ionising radiation there is no safe dose, so exposure is kept As Low As Reasonably Achievable with shielding, distance, time limits and monitoring. Reactivity explains secondary pollutants - sulfur dioxide oxidised to sulfuric acid, nitrogen oxides and hydrocarbons forming ozone and PANs in photochemical smog - so control targets the primary emissions with flue-gas desulfurisation and catalytic converters. Synergism between smoke and sulfur dioxide, or ozone and sulfur dioxide, means limits must consider pollutants together.</p><p>Properties interact with the environment: temperature inversions trap pollutants, currents disperse them, pH and oxygen alter solubility. Critical Pathway Analysis combines the two to predict where a discharge will concentrate and to focus monitoring, and Critical Group Monitoring checks the most exposed people. These principles have reduced smoke, sulfur dioxide, lead and organochlorines dramatically in developed countries. Their limits are pollutants with diffuse sources or that underpin economies - nitrate, carbon dioxide - where knowing the properties is not the problem; changing behaviour is. Even there, the precautionary principle applies the same logic: predict the properties of a new substance from similar ones and control it before release, because prevention is always cheaper than remediation.</p>",
        "notes": ["Every paragraph is organised around a <b>property</b>, each linked to a prediction and a named control - the essay answers the question throughout rather than describing pollutants.",
                  "Examples span the specification (smoke, acid rain, pesticides, heavy metals, radiation, nutrients, oil), with quantitative and named detail (Bhopal, Minamata, PM10, ALARA), which is what Level 5 breadth means.",
                  "The final paragraph evaluates: where the approach has worked, where it has not and why, and reaches a justified conclusion.",
                  "A Level 3 version of this essay would describe several pollutants and their effects accurately but only occasionally connect a property to a control, and would end without a judgement."]},
    "ESS-P2-CONSERVE": {
        "top_level": "Level 5 (23 marks)",
        "top": "<p>Biodiversity is lost through direct exploitation, habitat destruction, introduced species, pollution and changes to abiotic factors, so its conservation needs methods that address each cause, at scales from a single species to international law. Their effectiveness varies with the threat, the species and the resources available.</p><p>Deciding what to conserve comes first. The IUCN Red List uses population size, area, decline rate and probability of extinction to prioritise species, and endemic, keystone, flagship and evolutionarily distinct species receive weight because their loss has wider consequences. This is effective at directing scarce resources, but it favours well-studied vertebrates over invertebrates and plants.</p><p>Legislation and agreements protect species and habitats. In the UK the Wildlife and Countryside Act, SSSIs, NNRs and Marine Conservation Zones, and in Europe SACs, SPAs and the Habitats Directive, restrict damaging activities; internationally CITES controls trade in threatened species, the IWC regulates whaling, the Ramsar Convention protects wetlands and the Rio Convention on Biological Diversity commits nations to conservation. Their effectiveness depends on enforcement: CITES has reduced trade in ivory and rhino horn but poaching continues where enforcement is weak; the IWC moratorium allowed some whale populations to recover but Japan and Norway continued whaling; designations protect against development but not against pollution or climate change crossing boundaries.</p><p>In-situ habitat conservation and management are the most fundamental methods because they keep species with the community and abiotic conditions they depend on. Management uses ecological understanding: maintaining plagioclimaxes such as chalk grassland and heathland by grazing or burning, coppicing and pollarding woodland, controlling water levels, light and grazing, designing reserves with the right area, shape, biological corridors and buffer zones, and creating new habitats such as wetlands in worked-out gravel pits. Case studies show it works - coppiced woodland restored dormice and butterflies, wetland creation increased bitterns - but reserves are often small and fragmented and need permanent funding.</p><p>Ex-situ methods rescue species for which in-situ protection has failed. Captive breeding faces problems of space, diet, breeding triggers, social groups and small gene pools, addressed with studbooks, artificial insemination, embryo transfer and cryopreservation; seed banks such as the Millennium Seed Bank store plant genetic diversity cheaply. The Arabian oryx, California condor and pool frog show captive breeding can bring species back, but only where protected habitat exists for hard or soft release with post-release monitoring, and it is expensive and works for few species.</p><p>Ecological monitoring - image databases, eDNA, tracking, satellites - measures whether any of this is working and has improved the targeting of effort. Overall, legislation is effective when enforced, habitat management is the most effective and widely applicable method, and captive breeding is a last resort that succeeds only in combination with habitat protection. The Living Planet Index continues to fall, however, which shows that conservation methods are winning individual battles while the drivers of loss - population, consumption, land-use change and climate change - remain unaddressed.</p>",
        "notes": ["The essay is structured by <b>type of method</b> and evaluates the effectiveness of each in turn, with named examples for both successes and failures.",
                  "It draws on several specification areas (biodiversity, conservation legislation, habitat management, captive breeding, ecological processes, monitoring) - the synoptic breadth Level 5 requires.",
                  "The conclusion ranks the methods and then steps back to the bigger picture (Living Planet Index, underlying drivers) - a supported judgement rather than a summary.",
                  "A Level 3 essay would describe the same methods accurately but with fewer examples, little evaluation of enforcement or cost, and a conclusion that all methods are important."]},
}
